                "new_text": "Then optionally run the rest of the notebook top-to-bottom (`run_all_cells`) to make sure downstream cells (which rely on `cfg.data.mask_dir` indirectly through `FlareDSDataset`) still behave as before.\n\n---\n\n# Fix wrong kernel on 1_baseline_template.ipynb\n\n## Context\n\nCell `4ed04321` in `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/1_baseline_template.ipynb` fails with:\n\n```\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'\n```\n\nThis is a kernel misconfiguration, not a code bug. `0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb` (in the same directory, works fine) has kernelspec metadata:\n\n```json\n{\"display_name\": \"Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py\"}\n```\n\n`1_baseline_template.ipynb` instead has:\n\n```json\n{\"display_name\": \"Python 3 (ipykernel)\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"python3\"}\n```\n\n`name: python3` resolves to the base `notebook` conda env (`/srv/conda/envs/notebook`), which has no `torch` installed. The correct env is `/home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws` (verified: `torch 2.9.1+cu128` present there), exposed to Jupyter as kernel `conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py` via `nb_conda_kernels` (confirmed installed and active in the `notebook` env). This is exactly the kernel notebook 0 already uses successfully.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. Edit `1_baseline_template.ipynb`'s notebook-level `metadata.kernelspec` to match notebook 0's:\n   ```json\n   {\"display_name\": \"Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py\"}\n   ```\n   Done directly on the JSON (kernelspec metadata isn't reachable through the cell-level `NotebookEdit`/Jupyter-MCP tools, which only edit cells).\n2. If the notebook is currently open with a live kernel session, editing the file's metadata won't reconnect an already-running session \u2014 switch the running kernel via the Jupyter UI (\"Change Kernel\" \u2192 `Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]`), or restart the notebook's kernel through the MCP tools so it picks up the new kernelspec.\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter switching kernels, re-run cell `4ed04321` (and ideally the notebook from the top via `run_all_cells`) and confirm no `ModuleNotFoundError` \u2014 the `torch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')` line at the end of that cell should execute with no output/error."
