            "raw_input": {
              "planFilePath": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/adi-wdir-surya-workshop-downstream-apps-floofy-glacier.md",
              "plan": "# Fix wrong config import in sf_triggers notebook\n\n## Context\n\n`downstream_apps/sf_triggers/0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb` cell `63c1fe60` fails with:\n\n```\nValueError: Unrecognized key(s) in 'data:' section of the config: mask_dir, mask_time_tolerance.\nValid keys for FlareDataConfig are: channels, ds_match_direction, ... (no mask_dir/mask_time_tolerance)\n```\n\n`configs/config_script.yaml` legitimately sets `mask_dir` and `mask_time_tolerance` under `data:` \u2014 these drive the SHARP bitmap mask alignment used later in the notebook. The sf_triggers app already has its own `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/configs.py::FlareDataConfig`, which *does* declare both fields (and adds `mask_dir` to `PATH_FIELDS` for path resolution). The notebook cell, however, imports `load_flare_config` from `downstream_apps.template.configs` \u2014 the generic template's version, which only has the original four flare-alignment fields and knows nothing about masks. That mismatch is the entire bug; every other cell in the notebook (e.g. the `FlareDSDataset` import) already correctly points at the `sf_triggers` app.\n\n## Fix\n\nIn `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb`, cell `63c1fe60`, change:\n\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.template.configs import load_flare_config\n```\n\nto:\n\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.sf_triggers.configs import load_flare_config\n```\n\nNo other changes needed \u2014 `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/configs.py` already has the right fields.\n\n## Verification\n\nRun the notebook cell(s) via the Jupyter MCP tools (`read_notebook_cells` to confirm the edit landed, then `run_cell` on `63c1fe60`) and confirm:\n- No `ValueError` is raised.\n- `Loaded config for job: solar_flare_forecasting` prints, along with resolved train index / scalers paths.\n\nThen optionally run the rest of the notebook top-to-bottom (`run_all_cells`) to make sure downstream cells (which rely on `cfg.data.mask_dir` indirectly through `FlareDSDataset`) still behave as before.\n\n---\n\n# Fix wrong kernel on 1_baseline_template.ipynb\n\n## Context\n\nCell `4ed04321` in `downstream_apps/sf_triggers/1_baseline_template.ipynb` fails with:\n\n```\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'\n```\n\nThis is a kernel misconfiguration, not a code bug. `0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb` (in the same directory, works fine) has kernelspec metadata:\n\n```json\n{\"display_name\": \"Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py\"}\n```\n\n`1_baseline_template.ipynb` instead has:\n\n```json\n{\"display_name\": \"Python 3 (ipykernel)\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"python3\"}\n```\n\n`name: python3` resolves to the base `notebook` conda env (`/srv/conda/envs/notebook`), which has no `torch` installed. The correct env is `/home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws` (verified: `torch 2.9.1+cu128` present there), exposed to Jupyter as kernel `conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py` via `nb_conda_kernels` (confirmed installed and active in the `notebook` env). This is exactly the kernel notebook 0 already uses successfully.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. Edit `1_baseline_template.ipynb`'s notebook-level `metadata.kernelspec` to match notebook 0's:\n   ```json\n   {\"display_name\": \"Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"conda-env-jovyan-surya_ws-py\"}\n   ```\n   Done directly on the JSON (kernelspec metadata isn't reachable through the cell-level `NotebookEdit`/Jupyter-MCP tools, which only edit cells).\n2. If the notebook is currently open with a live kernel session, editing the file's metadata won't reconnect an already-running session \u2014 switch the running kernel via the Jupyter UI (\"Change Kernel\" \u2192 `Python [conda env:jovyan-surya_ws]`), or restart the notebook's kernel through the MCP tools so it picks up the new kernelspec.\n\n## Verification\n\nAfter switching kernels, re-run cell `4ed04321` (and ideally the notebook from the top via `run_all_cells`) and confirm no `ModuleNotFoundError` \u2014 the `torch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')` line at the end of that cell should execute with no output/error.\n"
            },
